{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And he drew near to him.</b> There is a fine point to examine: why was it necessary to say <b>וַיִּגַּשׁ</b> when he was already close to him and had been speaking with him until now? And our Sages of blessed memory interpreted it — one should examine their words there (Genesis Rabbah 93:6) — and we need to examine the plain sense of the verse. Further, there was no need to say the word <b>אֵלָיו</b>, for the matter is self-understood. Further, one must examine his saying <b>בִּי אֲדֹנִי</b> — the intent is not known, for what purpose; and if it was lest his anger burn, he should have said it next to ״וְאַל יִחַר״ etc. Further, one must examine his saying <b>בְּאָזְנֵי אֲדֹנִי</b>, and our Sages of blessed memory (ibid.) said that his words should enter his ears — but this is the way of derash. Further, one must examine the intent of his saying <b>כִּי כָמוֹךָ</b>, for it is not known what this reason attaches to; and on the contrary, since he is as great as Pharaoh, he would be particular about a slight in the conduct of honor and grandeur." "<b>Now</b> the plain sense of the verse is this: for it is a well-known matter that the custom of kings is that the great ones of the kingdom, their officers, and their counselors sit before them. And it was so that if a man came about a matter of judgment or a matter from the king, he would not stand interrupting between the king and his officers who sit first in the kingdom; rather, outside the circle he would stand, and there he would speak. And so Judah had been speaking until now, and afterwards ״וַיִּגַּשׁ אֵלָיו״ — meaning, he entered within his partition and stood between the king and between the officers, so that his words would not be heard by others beside the king." "And see what Maharam Alashkar wrote (in his responsa, siman 93) regarding the change of the word ״לוֹ״ to the word ״אֵלָיו״." "And he mollified the king's face so that he would be willing to let him speak with him in private — and this is his saying <b>בִּי אֲדֹנִי יְדַבֶּר נָא עַבְדְּךָ דָבָר בְּאָזְנֵי אֲדֹנִי</b> — with no hearer besides you, and this is impossible unless into his ears. And he mollified his face lest he hasten to become angry over one who asks such a thing and is not of the inner circle — and this is his saying <b>וְאַל יִחַר אַפְּךָ בְּעַבְדֶּךָ.</b> Meaning: for this is the way great men who are equals in this rank of greatness do toward one another — not a slave toward his master — and this is his saying <b>בְּעַבְדֶּךָ.</b> And the reason for the matter, ״כִּי כָמוֹךָ כְּפַרְעֹה״, meaning, along the lines of the incident brought in the Gemara (Avodah Zarah 10a) concerning Keti'a bar Shalom, of whom they said ״נָצַחְתְּ לְמַלְכָּא וְכָל דְּנָצַח לְמַלְכָּא״ etc. — up to here. Therefore, if Judah were to prevail over Joseph in the presence of the listeners, he would incur the danger of death by the law of one who prevails over the king. And lest one claim that the king is Pharaoh, therefore he said <b>כִּי כָמוֹךָ כְּפַרְעֹה</b> — and their law is equal; therefore he seeks, for his own life, to speak in his ear. And with this, even though he may prevail over him with arguments, there is no disgrace to the king, and he does not incur the law of one who prevails over the king who is liable — since it is between him and the king alone." "<b>Further</b>, it may intend <b>כִּי כָמוֹךָ</b> etc. along the lines of what is said (Proverbs 21:1) ״לֵב מֶלֶךְ בְּיַד ה׳״ — and if he were to speak in a manner such that his word would be heard by all who were present, they would turn the king's mind and will to the contrary, and the king would be found doing something other than what HaShem had placed in his heart, and his counselors would pervert his heart. Therefore let his anger not burn over his entering within his partition to speak in the king's ear. In this too he informed him that he is a man of kindness, and only those admitted to his presence would enter under suspicion of a matter of enticement toward rebellion." "<b>And by way of</b> derash the verse's saying <b>and he drew near to him</b> is explained along the lines of what they said (Proverbs 27:19): ״כַּמַּיִם הַפָּנִים לַפָּנִים כֵּן לֵב הָאָדָם לָאָדָם״ — and therefore Judah devised to incline Joseph's heart toward him in mercy, and he drew close his mind and his will to him, to love him and cherish him, so that Joseph's mind would draw near to him to accept his words and his entreaty. And he was compelled to do so, for by nature the sons of Jacob would not cherish idolaters, for (Psalms 125:3) ״לֹא יָנוּחַ שֵׁבֶט הָרֶשַׁע עַל גּוֹרַל הַצַּדִּיקִים,״ and from the very temper of their souls they hate those who have the flesh of donkeys as their flesh — therefore he needed, for the moment, to reverse what was imprinted, and to draw him close in his heart and to restore him in the chambers of his heart." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that Judah did not draw near to speak but rather to that which was asked of him — meaning, to what they understood, that the decree did not come from HaShem, as I explained in the preceding parashah on the verse (Genesis 44:17) ״חָלִילָה.״ And since the matter came only from Joseph, and to him the matter is given over to do as he pleases — therefore ״וַיִּגַּשׁ״." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to say that he drew near for Joseph's own benefit, for in every detail of the service that the slave Judah would perform he would do more than Benjamin would, and that is the meaning of <b>and he drew near to him</b> — meaning, to press an argument that is for the king's benefit, and that is (Genesis 44:33) ״יֵשֶׁב נָא״ etc." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by saying <b>to him</b>, lest a person say: how does Judah contradict his own words, ״הִנֶּנּוּ עֲבָדִים גַּם אֲנַחְנוּ״ (Genesis 44:16)? Therefore he said that what he drew near to speak — the things said about the matter, ״יֵשֶׁב נָא עַבְדְּךָ״ etc. ״וְהַנַּעַר יַעַל עִם אֶחָיו״ — was to him, meaning, regarding Joseph's words, that being the ground he found to enter and speak these words that he said, ״חָלִילָה״ etc. But as for what Judah himself said, there is no response in his language to say what he said regarding the matter." "And the verse's saying <b>Please, my lord</b> intends three things: The first, lest Joseph say to him, why does he speak alone out of all of them — all the more so given that earlier Joseph recognized that Reuben was the firstborn, as it is written (Genesis 43:33) ״וַיֵּשְׁבוּ לְפָנָיו הַבְּכוֹר״ etc., and if so, he would say: why does this one speak so much and not his brothers? Therefore he said, ״בִּי״ — the matter concerns me, as he explains to him at the end. And since he feared lest he become angry before the matter reached him, therefore he immediately prefaced and informed him, and that is what he said: ״בִּי נוֹגֵעַ הַדָּבָר אֲדוֹנִי.״ The second intention is to press an argument that could be heard as rescuing Benjamin, and that is <b>Please, my lord</b> — meaning: I stole the goblet and I placed it in Benjamin's sack, and from now on the line of justice demands that he be taken, for Benjamin denies and Judah admits — he shall be the slave. The third, Scripture hinted that the sovereignty belongs to him, and this is the measure of the words: ״בִּי״ is dependent on the epithet of the word ״אֲדֹנִי,״ for he is the king. And the verse's saying <b>let speak now</b> — meaning, he should now speak with the language of servitude for the time being, but the matter will not stand thus, for Judah is the king and his throne shall be established forever." "<b>Further,</b> the verse alludes by saying <b>בִּי אֲדֹנִי</b> — meaning: through my mediation you attained this, for he is the one who brought about Yosef's mastery, having said (Genesis 37:27) ״לְכוּ וְנִמְכְּרֶנּוּ לַיִּשְׁמְעֵאלִים״, for from this the matter turned and Yosef attained the mastery."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {2 "Maharam Alashkar's responsa are numbered by print edition; siman 93 follows the standard Venice/Constantinople numbering."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:49:20.281612805Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}